Kind: ablation
Issue: #659
Takes out: the kernel address x86_64_frame_read() asks for, which the walk made in the handler takes its steps through since #750 -- a canonical one is enough again, as before efe21b2f, and a ring-3 trap's low frame pointer is read through the probe's space, where nothing maps it, inside the handler
Run: --entry 0 600 -smp 1
Over: 659-widen-user-rbp.patch
Expect: faulting address 0x0+10(00|08)([^0-9a-fA-F]|$)
Note: either word of the widened frame, 0x1000 or 0x1008: the fault lands on whichever the walk reads first, which is the step's order and not the property -- the probe's own loop read the return address first and faulted at 0x1008, x86_64_frame_read() reads the caller's frame pointer first and faults at 0x1000

diff --git a/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/trap/trap.c b/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/trap/trap.c
index feb3d728..13d492dd 100644
--- a/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/trap/trap.c
+++ b/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/trap/trap.c
@@ -706,7 +706,7 @@ const char *x86_64_frame_read(uint64_t rbp, uint64_t *next, uint64_t *ret)
 
 	if ((rbp & 7) != 0)
 		return "not aligned";
-	if (!va_is_kernel(rbp))
+	if (!va_is_canonical(rbp))
 		return "not a kernel address";
 
 	/* Both words of the frame have to be there before either is read. */
